#{[17592186045463 #edb/ref 10 :activity.type/lesson #edb/ref 17592186045416] [17592186045485 #edb/ref 10 :question.difficulty/easy #edb/ref 17592186045416] [17592186045487 #edb/ref 10 :question.difficulty/moderate #edb/ref 17592186045416] [17592186045493 #edb/ref 10 :answer-field.type/radio #edb/ref 17592186045416] [17592186045498 #edb/ref 10 :answer.type/math #edb/ref 17592186045416] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046201 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046202 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046203 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046204 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046205 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046206 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046207 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046208 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046209 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046210 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046211 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046212 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046213 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046214 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046215 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046216 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046217 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046218 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046219 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046220 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046221 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046222 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046223 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046224 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046225 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046226 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046227 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046228 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046704 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046721 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046728 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046741 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046762 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046793 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046836 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046870 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046875 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046893 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046941 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046975 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1007 "Mathematics for Machine Learning" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1008 "Our Mathematics for Machine Learning course provides a comprehensive foundation of the essential mathematical tools required to study modern machine learning.\nThis course is divided into three main categories: linear algebra, multivariable calculus, and probability & statistics. The linear algebra section covers crucial machine learning fundamentals such as matrices, vector spaces, diagonalization, projections, singular value decomposition, and regression, as well as dimensionality reduction techniques such as principal component analysis. The multivariable calculus section develops the tools needed for optimization and learning algorithms, including vector and matrix calculus. Finally, the probability and statistics section covers random variables, point estimation, maximum likelihood estimation, and confidence intervals.\nOn completing this course, students will be well-prepared for a university-level machine learning course that tackles concepts such as gradient descent, neural networks, backpropagation, support vector machines, naive Bayes classifiers, and Gaussian mixture models." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1009 "The course begins with foundational topics in set theory and logic. Students develop a precise mathematical language for describing sets, functions, and predicates, and are introduced to key ideas such as supremum and infimum, as well as argmax and argmin notation. Logical reasoning and Boolean functions are also studied, providing important conceptual motivation for understanding the expressive limitations of some simple machine learning models, such as why certain functions (e.g. XOR) cannot be represented by a single-layer neural network.\nStudents then explore matrices in-depth. They study Gaussian elimination, solve systems of equations, learn about determinants and their properties, and compute inverse matrices. These tools form the computational backbone of many machine learning algorithms.\nAs part of this course, students perform a deep dive into vector spaces, exploring linear independence, subspaces, bases, dimension, rank, and nullity. They also study operations such as the Hadamard product, which appears naturally in element-wise computations in neural networks. Students generalize key concepts to abstract vector spaces and inner product spaces, and study orthogonality, projections, and the Gram–Schmidt process.\nStudents will learn how to find eigenvectors of a matrix, compute matrix diagonalizations, and extend these ideas to orthogonal diagonalization of symmetric matrices. These techniques underpin dimensionality reduction methods and are central to understanding the structure of data.\nThe course then develops key applications of linear algebra in machine learning. Students study singular value decomposition and its relationship to principal component analysis, and learn how these tools are used for dimensionality reduction, feature extraction, and identifying structure in high-dimensional datasets. Linear least-squares problems and regression techniques are also studied as foundational methods for fitting models to data.\nA solid grasp of multivariable calculus is essential for understanding modern machine learning algorithms. In this course, students become well-versed in partial derivatives and gradient vectors (used in gradient-based optimization), the multivariable chain rule (essential for backpropagation), and vector-valued functions. Students extend differentiation to maps between multi-dimensional vector spaces and develop the tools of vector and matrix calculus used to compute gradients of loss functions in neural networks.\nStudents also study optimization of multivariable functions, including critical points, second derivative tests, and constrained optimization using Lagrange multipliers. These techniques form the mathematical foundation of algorithms such as support vector machines and constrained optimization problems in machine learning.\nStudents work with standard multivariable surfaces to build intuition for loss surfaces, and study double integrals as a tool for understanding continuous probability distributions.\nOn the probability and statistics side, students study discrete and continuous random variables, including probability density functions, cumulative distribution functions, transformations, expectation, moments, variance, and Bayes' theorem. Important probability distributions are explored in detail.\nStudents extend their understanding to joint, marginal, and conditional distributions, as well as sums and products of random variables. Topics such as covariance, correlation, and multivariate normal distributions are introduced, which are essential in probabilistic modeling.\nThe course concludes with parametric inference, focusing on point estimation, maximum likelihood estimation, and confidence intervals, which form the statistical foundation for many machine learning methods." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1012 145 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1013 "MML" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1014 #uuid "1ee987ec-c9ff-5d2f-91f4-4de8079c0eac" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048944 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048389 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048944 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048755 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048944 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049257 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048944 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049718 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048944 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050435 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048944 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050461 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048944 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186188283 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048944 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186188284 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048944 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186188285 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048944 #edb/ref 1031 "Confidence Intervals for One Mean: Unknown Population Variance" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048944 #edb/ref 1033 3855 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048944 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "cdc1c937-d8fa-5ff7-934a-a282eda47b10" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050461 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186192682 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050461 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186192683 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050461 #edb/ref 1031 "Confidence Intervals for Linear Regression Intercept Parameters" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050461 #edb/ref 1033 4116 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050461 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "4b1a1cce-e35a-540f-9c78-90e0889cdb1f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050604 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048755 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050604 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050461 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050604 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186096822 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050604 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186096823 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050604 #edb/ref 1031 "Linear Regression" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050604 #edb/ref 1033 1057 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050604 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "57cd3867-de9d-5ea0-b2c4-74cd8e11123f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189760 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186553976 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189760 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186554226 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189760 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186554248 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189760 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186554389 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189760 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186554398 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189760 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189760 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189760 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186554248 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189760 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "2f9df6ed-4f2d-5912-b30b-a8d79df169ce" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192387 #edb/ref 1069 "e-10095" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192387 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $\\widehat{\\,y}=\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0+\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1x=1.6+0.7  x$ be the regression line fitted using a sample $(x_1,y_1), \\: \\ldots, \\: (x_{8},y_{8})$ of $n=8$ paired observations. Assuming that all conditions for the simple regression model are satisfied, $\\overline{x}=2.4,$ the mean square error of the model is $1.25,$ and $S_{xx} = \\sum_{i=1}^n (x_i - \\overline{x})^2 = 42,$ find a $90\\%$ confidence interval for the $y$-intercept of the regression line.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T > 1.943) = 0.05,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $6$ degrees of freedom. Also, the standard error for $\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0\\,] = \\sqrt{ \\textrm{MSE} \\cdot \\bigg( \\dfrac{1}{n} + \\dfrac{\\overline{x}^2}{S_{xx}} \\bigg) }\n$$\n\n*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192387 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c0233eba-1101-5c9f-8603-bbfdabb3923f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192387 #edb/ref 1074 "We use the following notations:\n\n- $y=\\beta_0 + \\beta_1 x$ is the regression line that best fits the population, and\n\n- $\\widehat{\\,y}=\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0+\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1x$ is the estimate of the \"true\" regression line given by the data in our sample.\n\nIn this case, we have that  \n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,y}=\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0+\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1x=1.6+0.7x.\n$$\n\nWe are told that all assumptions for a simple regression model are held. So, given a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha)]\\%$ confidence interval for the $y$-intercept $\\beta_0$ of our model can be written as\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0}_{\\:\\: \\textrm{point} \\ \\textrm{estimate}} \\: \\pm \\: \\overbrace{t_{n-2,\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0\\,]}^{\\textrm{margin of error}}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- $\\!\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0$ is our point estimate for the intercept, computed using the data points from our sample.\n\n- $P(T > t_{n-2,\\alpha/2}) = \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2},$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-2$ degrees of freedom.\n\n- $\\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0\\,]$ is the standard error of $\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0\\,] = \\sqrt{ \\textrm{MSE}\\cdot\\left(\\dfrac1n+\\dfrac{\\overline{x}^2}{S_{xx}}\\right)}\n$$\n\nwhere we have:\n\n- $\\textrm{MSE}$ is the mean squared error of the model:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{MSE} = \\dfrac{1}{n-2}\\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n (y_i - \\widehat{\\,y}_i)^2\n$$\n\nwhere $\\widehat{\\,y}_i$ is the estimate of $y_i$ given by our regression model.\n\n- $S_{xx}$ is the usual sum of squares formula:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle S_{xx} = \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n (x_i - \\overline{x})^2\n$$\n\nTo construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate. From the regression equation $\\widehat{\\,y}=1.6+0.7x,$ our point estimate is\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0 = 1.6.\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $90\\%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha=1-0.90=0.1 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n\\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T > 1.943) = 0.05.$ As a result, \n\n$$\nt_{n-2,\\alpha/2} = t_{6,0.05} = 1.943.\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\nt_{n-2,\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0\\,]  = 1.943 \\cdot \\sqrt{1.25 \\cdot \\bigg( \\dfrac{1}{8} + \\dfrac{2.4^2}{42} \\bigg)} \\approx 1.1\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Determine the limits of the confidence interval:\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0 \\: \\pm \\: t_{n-2,\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0\\,] =  1.6 \\pm 1.1\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192388 #edb/ref 1069 "e-10096" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192388 #edb/ref 1071 "| **Hours ($x$)** | **$4$** | **$4.5$** | **$5.5$** | **$6$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **Grades ($y$)** | $62$ | $65$ | $77$ | $81$ |\n\nThe table above shows the number of hours each of four students spent studying for a biology exam and the grades they obtained on the exam. The corresponding fitted linear regression line using this sample is $\\widehat{\\,y} = 21.3 + 10x.$\nAssuming that all conditions for the simple regression model are satisfied, $\\overline{x}=5,$ and $\\textrm{MSE}=1.375,$ find a $90\\%$ confidence interval for the $y$-intercept of the regression line.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T > 2.920) = 0.05,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $2$ degrees of freedom. Also, the standard error for the $y$-intecept is*\n\n$$\n\\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0\\,] = \\sqrt{ \\textrm{MSE} \\cdot \\bigg( \\dfrac{1}{n} + \\dfrac{\\overline{x}^2}{S_{xx}} \\bigg) }.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192388 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "bac24078-f1fb-56ae-ac2b-baa86b3a75ef" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192388 #edb/ref 1074 "We use the following notations:\n\n- $y=\\beta_0 + \\beta_1 x$ is the regression line that best fits the population, and\n\n- $\\widehat{\\,y}=\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0+\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1x$ is the estimate of the \"true\" regression line given by the data in our sample.\n\nIn this case, we have that  \n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,y}=\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0+\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1x= 21.3 + 10x.\n$$\n\nWe are told that all assumptions for a simple regression model are held. So, given a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha)]\\%$ confidence interval for the $y$-intercept $\\beta_0$ of our model can be written as\n\n$$\n\\beta_0 \\: \\pm \\: \\overbrace{t_{n-2,\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0\\,]}^{\\textrm{margin of error}}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- $\\!\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0$ is our point estimate for the intercept, computed using the data points from our sample.\n\n- $P(T > t_{n-2,\\alpha/2}) = \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2},$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-2$ degrees of freedom.\n\n- $\\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0\\,]$ is the standard error of $\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0\\,] = \\sqrt{ \\textrm{MSE}\\cdot\\left(\\dfrac1n+\\dfrac{\\overline{x}^2}{S_{xx}}\\right)}\n$$\n\nwhere we have:\n\n- $\\textrm{MSE}$ is the mean squared error of the model:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{MSE} = \\dfrac{1}{n-2}\\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n (y_i - \\widehat{\\,y}_i)^2\n$$\n\nwhere $\\widehat{\\,y}_i$ is the estimate of $y_i$ given by our regression model.\n\n- $S_{xx}$ is the usual sum of squares formula:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle S_{xx} = \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n (x_i - \\overline{x})^2\n$$\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate. From the regression equation $\\widehat{\\,y}=21.3 + 10x,$ our point estimate is\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0 = 21.3.\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. \n\n- Since we are interested in finding a $90\\%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha=1-0.9=0.1 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n\\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T > 2.920) = 0.05.$ As a result, \n\n$$\nt_{n-2,\\alpha/2} = t_{2,0.005} = 2.920.\n$$\n\n- Computing $S_{xx},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2} & =(4-5)^{2}+(4.5-5)^{2}+(5.5-5)^{2}+(6-5)^{2} \\\\  & =1+0.25+0.25+1 \\\\  & =2.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Now, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\nt_{n-2,\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0\\,]  = 2.920 \\cdot \\sqrt{1.375\\cdot \\bigg( \\dfrac{1}{4} + \\dfrac{5^2}{2.5} \\bigg)} \\approx 11.0\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Determine the limits of the confidence interval:\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0 \\: \\pm \\: t_{n-2,\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0\\,] =  21.3 \\pm 11.0\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192682 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048944 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192682 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050604 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192682 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186192979 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192682 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186193189 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192682 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186554550 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192682 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186554591 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192682 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186554594 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192682 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186554638 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192682 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding Confidence Intervals for Intercept Parameters" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192682 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186192387 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192682 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "7813df9e-9641-58a3-8537-e672e5fd079c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192683 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186192980 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192683 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186193188 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192683 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186554542 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192683 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186554572 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192683 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186554581 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192683 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186554599 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192683 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186554613 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192683 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding Confidence Intervals in Context" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192683 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186192388 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192683 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "4bbee7f0-8803-54e8-874c-e570323ea33a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192927 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186193503 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192927 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186193504 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192927 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186193676 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192927 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186193677 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192927 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186193678 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192927 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186193690 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192927 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192927 #edb/ref 1045 "Confidence Intervals for Linear Regression Intercept Parameters" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192927 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186193676 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192927 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050461 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192927 #edb/ref 1050 480.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192927 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "27b7cff9-86aa-5b81-a7d3-2697abb3c99e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192979 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186189760 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192979 #edb/ref 1069 "q-143256" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192979 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192979 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x=3.5+5x$ be the regression line fitted using a sample $({x}_{1},{y}_{1}),\\,…,\\,({x}_{20},{y}_{20})$ of $n=20$ paired observations. Assuming that all conditions for the simple regression model are satisfied, $\\overset{―}{x}=2,$ the mean square error of the model is $8,$ and ${S}_{xx}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2}=24,$ find a $95%$ confidence interval for the $y$-intercept of the regression line.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T>2.101)=0.025,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $18$ degrees of freedom. Also, the standard error for ${\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\text{MSE}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{n}+\\frac{{\\overset{―}{x}}^{2}}{{S}_{xx}})}\n$$\n\n*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192979 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "68d56eaf-9e11-54bc-9fcb-5131b64bc995" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192979 #edb/ref 1073 true #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192979 #edb/ref 1074 "We use the following notations:\n\n- $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ is the regression line that best fits the population, and\n\n- $\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x$ is the estimate of the \"true\" regression line given by the data in our sample.\n\nIn this case, we have that  \n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x=3.5+5x.\n$$\n\nWe are told that all assumptions for a simple regression model are held. So, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the $y$-intercept ${\\beta }_{0}$ of our model can be written as\n\n$$\n{{{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}}_{⏟}}_{\\,\\,\\text{point}\\,\\text{estimate}}\\,\\pm \\,\\overset{\\text{margin of error}}{\\overset{⏞}{{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]}}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- $\\,{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}$ is our point estimate for the intercept, computed using the data points from our sample.\n\n- $P(T>{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-2$ degrees of freedom.\n\n- $\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]$ is the standard error of ${\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0},$ given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\text{MSE}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{n}+\\frac{{\\overset{―}{x}}^{2}}{{S}_{xx}})}\n$$\n\nwhere we have:\n\n- $\\text{MSE}$ is the mean squared error of the model:\n\n$$\n\\text{MSE}=\\frac{1}{n-2}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({y}_{i}-{\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i})^{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i}$ is the estimate of ${y}_{i}$ given by our regression model.\n\n- ${S}_{xx}$ is the usual sum of squares formula:\n\n$$\n{S}_{xx}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2}\n$$\n\nTo construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate. From the regression equation $\\overset{^}{\\,y}=3.5+5x,$ our point estimate is\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}=3.5.\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $95%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.95=0.05\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T>2.101)=0.025.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}={t}_{18,0.025}=2.101.\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=2.101\\cdot \\sqrt{8\\cdot (\\frac{1}{20}+\\frac{{2}^{2}}{24})}≈2.8\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Determine the limits of the confidence interval:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,\\pm \\,{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=3.5\\pm 2.8\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193503 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186193504 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193503 #edb/ref 1061 38067 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193503 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b29450d6-7dae-513c-855c-9d127f25ad09" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193503 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186192682 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193504 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186193690 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193504 #edb/ref 1061 38068 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193504 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2db38e99-7855-5515-a956-dbad946ec6df" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193504 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186192683 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193676 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186193677 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193676 #edb/ref 1061 42156 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193676 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "140fd64b-240a-5128-bceb-5d2258cb5439" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193676 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186193891 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193677 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186193503 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193677 #edb/ref 1061 42158 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193677 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ac2be2f7-e3fa-5b92-b417-ac33fa6ff9d7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193677 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186193892 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193678 #edb/ref 1061 42159 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193678 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2e9c0fd9-3132-5c7d-b2e2-2b04a2530995" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193678 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186193893 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193690 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186193678 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193690 #edb/ref 1061 42309 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193690 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e07f8b0d-114e-5676-8692-12b109c4375f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193690 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186193900 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193891 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose we conduct a random sample of $n$ of *paired* observations $(x_i,y_i)$ drawn from a population. \n\n$$\n(x_1,y_1), \\quad (x_2,y_2), \\quad \\ldots, \\quad (x_n,y_n)\n$$\n\nUsing previously discussed techniques, we can calculate the linear regression line for this data, which we'll denote as\n\n$$\ny = \\widehat{\\beta_0} + \\widehat{\\beta_1} x.\n$$\n\nOur *estimates* for the **slope parameter** $\\widehat{\\beta_1}$ and **intercept parameter** $\\widehat{\\beta_0}$  are given by\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\beta_1} = \\dfrac{S_{xy}}{S_{xx}}, \\qquad \\widehat{\\beta_0} = \\overline{y} -  \\widehat{\\beta_1}\\overline{x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\overline{x}$ and $\\overline{y}$ are the mean values of $x_i$ and $y_i,$ and the quantities $S_{xx}$ and $S_{xy}$ are given by\n\n$$\nS_{xx} = \\sum_{i=1}^n (x_i-\\overline{x})^2, \n\\qquad S_{xy} = \\sum_{i=1}^n (x_i-\\overline{x})(y_i-\\overline{y}).\n$$\n\nPlotting our regression line alongside the sample data, we might get a diagram similar to the following: \n\n![](images/68/681a96293905841b417dd7ca9faa2631aedc5ad57e4f2d95fcd40089b9e83c6e.png)\n\nNotice that we referred to $\\widehat{\\beta_0}$ and $\\widehat{\\beta_1}$ as *estimates*. This is because if we were to conduct a census on the population, we'd be able to calculate the \"true\" regression equation for the data that considers every population member. We'll denote this \"true\" regression line as \n\n$$\ny = \\beta_0 + \\beta_1 x.\n$$\n\nThe two lines are shown below.\n\n![](images/df/df08670fd3c4aff99e994692eff28a49a9ec47d4aa73185682e3e0e829e3d562.png)\n\nTo summarize, \n\n- the regression equation $y = \\widehat{\\beta_0} + \\widehat{\\beta_1} x$ is an estimate of the true regression equation $y = \\beta_0 + \\beta_1 x,$\n\n- $\\widehat{\\beta_1}$ is an estimate of the \"true\" slope parameter $\\beta_1,$ and\n\n- $\\widehat{\\beta_0}$ is an estimate of the \"true\" intercept parameter $\\beta_0.$\n\nIn this lesson, we will learn how to construct confidence intervals for the intercept parameter $\\beta_0.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193891 #edb/ref 1065 6965 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193891 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "109cb2d6-433c-5ebc-8c14-b0db564dcee8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193891 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193892 #edb/ref 1064 "Given a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha)]\\%$ confidence interval for the intercept $\\beta_0$ of our model can be written as\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0}_{\\:\\: \\textrm{point} \\ \\textrm{estimate}} \\: \\pm \\: \\overbrace{t_{n-2,\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\underbrace{\\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0\\,]}_{\\textrm{standard} \\ \\,\\:\\: \\textrm{error}}}^{\\textrm{margin of error}}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- $\\!\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0$ is our point estimate for the intercept, computed using the data points from our sample.\n\n- $P(T > t_{n-2,\\alpha/2}) = \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2},$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-2$ degrees of freedom.\n\n- $\\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0\\,]$ is the standard error of $\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0\\,] = \\sqrt{ \\textrm{MSE}\\cdot\\left(\\dfrac1n+\\dfrac{\\overline{x}^2}{S_{xx}}\\right)}\n$$\n\nwhere we have:\n\n- $\\textrm{MSE}$ is the mean squared error of the model:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{MSE} = \\dfrac{1}{n-2}\\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n (y_i - \\widehat{\\,y}_i)^2\n$$\n\nwhere $\\widehat{\\,y}_i$ is the estimate of $y_i$ given by our regression model.\n\n- $S_{xx}$ is the usual sum of squares formula:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle S_{xx} = \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n (x_i - \\overline{x})^2\n$$\n\nThe derivation of this result is rather long, and we leave it until the end of the lesson. For now, note the following:\n\n- Each data point in the sample has some *residual* (i.e., error) associated with it. The error of our regression model at the data point $(x_i,y_i)$ is given by \n\n$$\n(y_i - \\widehat{\\,y}_i).\n$$\n\n- The expression $\\displaystyle\\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n (y_i - \\widehat{\\,y}_i)^2$ gives the sum of the squared residuals of our regression model.\n\n- Even considering the entire population, the data points will not fall on a perfectly straight line. Thus, errors are present even in the \"true\" regression model. These errors have some (unknown) variance, which we'll denote as $\\sigma^2.$\n\n- It can be shown that the $\\textrm{MSE}$ is an *unbiased* estimator of $\\sigma^2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193892 #edb/ref 1065 6967 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193892 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "cc1aaf89-d357-53d7-8943-926609018212" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193892 #edb/ref 1067 "Summary of the Key Result" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193893 #edb/ref 1064 "Earlier, we made use of the following results:\n\n$$\n\\textrm E[\\widehat{\\beta}_0] = \\beta_0,\n\\qquad\n\\textrm{Var}[\\widehat{\\beta_0}] = \\sigma^2\\left(\\dfrac{1}{n} + \\dfrac{\\overline{x}^2}{S_{xx}}\\right)\n$$\n\nLet's now derive these. First, we will show that\n\n$$\nS_{xy} = \\sum_{i=1}^n (x_i-\\overline{x})y_i.\n$$\n\nWe can show this is true using some straightforward manipulations of the summation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xy} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x}){y}_{i}-{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})\\overset{―}{y} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x}){y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x}){y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}+\\overset{―}{y}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\overset{―}{x} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x}){y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}+\\overset{―}{y}n\\overset{―}{x} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x}){y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}+\\overset{―}{y}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x}){y}_{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we define $k_i$ as\n\n$$\nk_i = \\dfrac{ x_i-\\overline{x}}{S_{xx}}.\n$$\n\nThe following properties can be shown using straightforward algebra:\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\sum_{i=1}^n k_i=0$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\sum_{i=1}^n k_i x_i=1$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\sum_{i=1}^n k_i^2=\\dfrac{1}{S_{xx}}$\n\nNow, recall that\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\beta_0} = \\overline{y} - \\widehat{\\beta_1}\\overline{x}.\n$$\n\nWe can rewrite our expression for $\\widehat{\\beta_0}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{{\\beta }_{0}} & =\\overset{―}{y}-{\\beta }_{1}\\overset{―}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{y}_{i}-\\frac{{S}_{xy}}{{S}_{xx}}\\overset{―}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{y}_{i}-\\frac{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x}){y}_{i}}{{S}_{xx}}\\overset{―}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{y}_{i}-{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{k}_{i}{y}_{i}\\overset{―}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{k}_{i}{y}_{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're now in a position to compute $\\textrm{E}[{\\widehat{\\beta_0} }]$ and $\\textrm{Var}[{\\widehat{\\beta_0} }].$\n\n- For the mean, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{{\\beta }_{0}}] & =\\frac{1}{n}\\text{E}[{\\sum }_{i}{y}_{i}]-\\overset{―}{x}\\text{E}[{\\sum }_{i}{k}_{i}{y}_{i}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i}\\text{E}[{y}_{i}]-\\overset{―}{x}{\\sum }_{i}\\text{E}[{k}_{i}{y}_{i}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i}({\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}{x}_{i})-\\overset{―}{x}{\\sum }_{i}{k}_{i}({\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}{x}_{i}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i}({\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}{x}_{i})-\\overset{―}{x}[{\\beta }_{0}{\\sum }_{i}{k}_{i}+{\\beta }_{1}{\\sum }_{i}{k}_{i}{x}_{i}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i}({\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}{x}_{i})-\\overset{―}{x}[{\\beta }_{0}{\\cancel{{\\sum }_{i}{k}_{i}}}^{0}+{\\beta }_{1}{\\cancel{{\\sum }_{i}{k}_{i}{x}_{i}}}^{1}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i}({\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}{x}_{i})-\\overset{―}{x}{\\beta }_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\beta }_{0}}{n}{\\sum }_{i}1+\\frac{{\\beta }_{1}}{n}{\\sum }_{i}{x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x}{\\beta }_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\beta }_{0}}{n}\\cdot n+\\overset{―}{x}{\\beta }_{1}-\\overset{―}{x}{\\beta }_{1} \\\\  & ={\\beta }_{0}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the variance, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{{\\beta }_{0}}] & =\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}}\\text{Var}[{\\sum }_{i}{y}_{i}]+{\\overset{―}{x}}^{2}\\text{Var}[{\\sum }_{i}{k}_{i}{y}_{i}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}}{\\sum }_{i}\\text{Var}[{y}_{i}]+{\\overset{―}{x}}^{2}{\\sum }_{i}{k}_{i}^{2}\\text{Var}[{y}_{i}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}}n{\\sigma }^{2}+{\\overset{―}{x}}^{2}{\\sum }_{i}{k}_{i}^{2}{\\sigma }^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}}n{\\sigma }^{2}+{\\overset{―}{x}}^{2}{\\sigma }^{2}{\\sum }_{i}{k}_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sigma }^{2}+{\\overset{―}{x}}^{2}{\\sigma }^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{S}_{xx}} \\\\  & ={\\sigma }^{2}(\\frac{1}{n}+\\frac{{\\overset{―}{x}}^{2}}{{S}_{xx}}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo finally, we have\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\beta_0} \\sim N\\left(\\beta_0,\\sigma^2\\left(\\dfrac{1}{n} + \\dfrac{\\overline{x}^2}{S_{xx}}\\right)\\right).\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193893 #edb/ref 1065 6968 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193893 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "9e3cbadc-9a0c-5925-af3f-b47ca4a2ff80" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193893 #edb/ref 1067 "Expectation and Variance of Intercept Parameter Estimates" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193900 #edb/ref 1064 "We'll now derive our expression for the confidence interval for our intercept parameter estimate $\\widehat{\\beta_0}.$\n\nSuppose we conduct a random sample of $n$ of *paired* observations $(x_i,y_i).$\n\n$$\n(x_1,y_1), \\quad (x_2,y_2), \\quad \\ldots, \\quad (x_n,y_n)\n$$\n\nLet's assume that our \"global\" regression line (i.e., the line that best fits the entire population) is given by\n\n$$\ny = \\beta_0 + \\beta_1 x.\n$$\n\nFor each $i=1,2,\\ldots,n,$ let\n\n$$\ny_i = \\beta_0 + \\beta_1 x_i + \\epsilon_i,\n$$\n\nwhere we assume that the error terms $\\epsilon_i$ are I.I.D, and \n\n$$\n\\epsilon_i \\sim N(0, \\sigma^2)\n$$\n\nfor some unknown variance $\\sigma^2.$ The error terms and their distribution can be visualized as shown below.\n\n![](images/69/69135b6cdc4d34f5422da39bac5d15624d16f4817e91f282974507c711953796.png)\n\nThis means that each $y_i$ is a normally distributed random variable with\n\n$$\ny_i \\sim N\\left( \\beta_0 + \\beta_1 x_i , \\sigma^2\\right).\n$$\n\nIt can be shown that \n\n$$\n\\textrm E[\\widehat{\\beta}_0] = \\beta_0,\n\\qquad\n\\textrm{Var}[\\widehat{\\beta_0}] =  \\sigma^2\\left(\\dfrac1n+ \\dfrac{\\overline{x}^2}{S_{xx}}\\right).\n$$\n\nWe'll derive these results separately in a while. But for now, we'll simply state them.\n\nUsing these results, we can express the distribution of $\\widehat{\\beta_0}$ as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\beta}_0 \\sim N\\left(\\beta_0, \\: \\sigma^2\\left(\\dfrac1n+ \\dfrac{\\overline{x}^2}{S_{xx}}\\right) \\right).\n$$\n\nWe do not know $\\sigma^2,$ the variance of the error terms. However, it can be shown that the mean squared error, given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{MSE} = \\dfrac{1}{n-2}\\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n (y_i - \\widehat{\\,y}_i)^2,\n$$\n\nis an unbiased estimator of $\\sigma^2.$\n\nOur estimate of the standard error of $\\widehat{\\beta}_0$ is now given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\beta}_0] = \\sqrt{\\textrm{MSE}\\cdot\\left(\\dfrac1n+ \\dfrac{\\overline{x}^2}{S_{xx}}\\right)} .\n$$\n\nSince this is an *estimate* of the standard error, it can be shown that the random variable\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\widehat{\\beta}_0 -\\beta_0}{\\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\beta_0}] }\n$$\n\nfollows a student's $t$-distribution with $n-2$ degrees of freedom. \n\nFinally, given a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha)]\\%$ confidence interval for the intercept $\\beta_0$ of our model can be written as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0 \\: \\pm \\: \\overbrace{t_{n-2,\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0\\,] }^{\\textrm{margin of error}}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193900 #edb/ref 1065 7007 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193900 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "1aef59f3-040d-59b5-90f2-5f746d059c4a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193900 #edb/ref 1067 "Deriving an Expression for the Confidence Interval" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553910 #edb/ref 1080 "-26.6\\pm 18.1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553910 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553910 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "15060daf-a4bc-5eb8-b79b-034a85e0bbf7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553918 #edb/ref 1080 "5\\pm 2.3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553918 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553918 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "19ea48dc-2bbd-5e4f-9e61-02d95414ac74" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553937 #edb/ref 1080 "2.8\\pm 1.1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553937 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553937 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "25f6ae7e-79db-5a6a-9ada-f7fe426e2d90" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553938 #edb/ref 1080 "-165\\pm 44.7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553938 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553938 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "2629af92-040b-52b8-9b80-03dbd9c188f3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553976 #edb/ref 1080 "3.5\\pm 3.0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553976 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553976 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "3b4ae633-d836-59a7-8030-002829769a32" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553977 #edb/ref 1080 "12.6\\pm 1.3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553977 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553977 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "3b96016e-6c70-5725-be4a-7c141b414f3f" #edb/ref 1