ha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-2$ degrees of freedom.\n\n- $\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]$ is the standard error of ${\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0},$ given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\text{MSE}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{n}+\\frac{{\\overset{―}{x}}^{2}}{{S}_{xx}})}\n$$\n\nwhere we have:\n\n- $\\text{MSE}$ is the mean squared error of the model:\n\n$$\n\\text{MSE}=\\frac{1}{n-2}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({y}_{i}-{\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i})^{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i}$ is the estimate of ${y}_{i}$ given by our regression model.\n\n- ${S}_{xx}$ is the usual sum of squares formula:\n\n$$\n{S}_{xx}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2}\n$$\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate. From the regression equation $\\overset{^}{\\,y}=0.6+0.2x,$ our point estimate is\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}=0.6.\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. \n\n- Since we are interested in finding a $80%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.8=0.2\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.1.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T>1.886)=0.1.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}={t}_{2,0.1}=1.886.\n$$\n\n- Computing ${S}_{xx},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2} & =(34-38)^{2}+(36-38)^{2}+(40-38)^{2}+(42-38)^{2} \\\\  & =16+4+4+16 \\\\  & =40.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Now, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=1.886\\cdot \\sqrt{0.0125\\cdot (\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{{38}^{2}}{40})}≈1.3\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Determine the limits of the confidence interval:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,\\pm \\,{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=0.6\\pm 1.3\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554581 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186554467 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554581 #edb/ref 1069 "q-159557" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [1759218