4+16 \\\\  & =40.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Now, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=6.965\\cdot \\sqrt{7.7\\cdot (\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{{64}^{2}}{40})}≈195.8\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Determine the limits of the confidence interval:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,\\pm \\,{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=151.3\\pm 195.8\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554638 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186554432 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554638 #edb/ref 1069 "q-159563" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554638 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554638 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x=2.8-3.3x$ be the regression line fitted using a sample $({x}_{1},{y}_{1}),\\,…,\\,({x}_{10},{y}_{10})$ of $n=10$ paired observations. Assuming that all conditions for the simple regression model are satisfied, $\\overset{―}{x}=2.7,$ the mean square error of the model is $3.6,$ and ${S}_{xx}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2}=82.5,$ find a $99%$ confidence interval for the $y$-intercept of the regression line.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T>3.355)=0.005,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $8$ degrees of freedom. Also, the standard error for ${\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\text{MSE}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{n}+\\frac{{\\overset{―}{x}}^{2}}{{S}_{xx}})}\n$$\n\n*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554638 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f24d9e6f-29a8-5c79-a6cb-e6a041461fd9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554638 #edb/ref 1074 "We use the following notations:\n\n- $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ is the regression line that best fits the population, and\n\n- $\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x$ is the estimate of the \"true\" regression line g